\label{tab:selKinEvt} \end{table} }
The signal and control regions of the resolved analysis are obtained based on selection requirements on the $w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT}$ observable. A dedicated signal and control region is defined for each of the four $5j3b$, $5j\geq4b$, $\geq6j3b$, and $\geq6j\geq4b$ categories. Hence, eight regions are used in the resolved $\ell^\pm\nu b\bar{b}$ channel: \begin{itemize} \item \emph{Signal regions (SRs)} are defined by $w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT} \geq 0.7$, \item \emph{Control regions (CRs)} are defined in the range of $-0.5 \leq w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT} < 0.5$. \end{itemize}
Twelve regions are used in the resolved $q\bar{q} b\bar{b}$ channel: \begin{itemize} \item \emph{High-purity signal regions} are defined by $w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT} \geq 0.9$, \item \emph{Low-purity signal regions} are defined by $0.0 \leq w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT} < 0.9$ for events with exactly three $b$-tagged jets and by $0.6 \leq w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT} < 0.9$ for events with at least four $b$-tagged jets, \item \emph{Control regions} are defined in the range of $-0.5 \leq w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT} < 0.0$ for events with exactly three $b$-tagged jets and by $-0.5 \leq w^\mathrm{max}_\mathrm{BDT} < 0.6$ for events with at least four $b$-tagged jets. \end{itemize}
Events enter the signal regions of the merged analysis, if the mass of the $h\rightarrow b\bar{b}$ tagged large-$R$ jet ($m_{h}$) is in a window around the Higgs boson pole mass, $95\,\GeV \leq m_{h} < 140\,\GeV$. The signal regions are further split based on the NN output score. A dedicated signal region is defined for each of the $0b$ and $\geq1b$ categories. Hence, six regions are used for the merged $\ell^\pm\nu b\bar{b}$ channel: \begin{itemize} \item \emph{High-NN-score signal regions} are defined by $w_\mathrm{NN} \geq 0.83$, \item \emph{Medium-NN-score signal regions} are defined by $0.4\leq w_\mathrm{NN} < 0.83$, \item \emph{Low-NN-score signal regions} are defined by $w_\mathrm{NN}$ < 0.4. \end{itemize}
In the merged $q\bar{q}b\bar{b}$ channel four regions are used: \begin{itemize} \item \emph{High-NN-score signal regions} are defined by $w_\mathrm{NN} \geq 0.2$ ($w_\mathrm{NN} \geq 0.1$) for events with (without) additional $b$-tagged jets, \item \emph{Low-NN-score signal regions} are defined by $w_\mathrm{NN} < 0.2$ ($w_\mathrm{NN} < 0.1$) for events with (without) additional $b$-tagged jets. \end{itemize}
Furthermore, two sets of sideband control regions are defined in the merged event categories by inverting the selection requirement on $m_{h}$. \emph{Low-mass sidebands} ($50\,\GeV \leq m_{h}< 95\,\GeV$) are defined to constrain the $W$+jets background, while \emph{high-mass sidebands} ($140\,\GeV \leq m_{h}< 250\,\GeV$) are defined to target backgrounds containing boosted top quarks. Again, a dedicated region is defined per $0b$ and $\geq1b$ category. The various signal and control regions are summarised in table~\ref{tab:AnalysisRegionsFullTable}.
The products of kinematic acceptance and reconstruction efficiency for \mbox{$pp\rightarrow tb H^\pm(\rightarrow W^\pm h)$} is presented in figures~\ref{fig:SignalSelectionEfficienciesResolved} and~\ref{fig:SignalSelectionEfficienciesMerged} separately for all signal regions of the resolved and merged $\ell^\pm\nu b\bar{b}$ and $q\bar{q}b\bar{b}$ decay channels. In this context, the acceptance is defined as the fraction of simulated signal events for which the expected final state particles satisfy all relevant object definition requirements. The denominator of the acceptance is calculated considering simulated signal events with inclusive decays of the $W$ boson, and $h\rightarrow b\bar{b}$ decays of the $125\,\GeV$ Higgs boson. The reconstruction efficiency is defined as the ratio of simulated signal events that satisfy all selection criteria for a given signal region to the total number of simulated signal events that satisfy the acceptance requirements.
